WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
Movie fans might have noticed that Jackie Chan&nbsp;
has appeared in fewer and fewer American films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.440
throughout the years. After he became one&nbsp;
of Hong Kong's biggest and most profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.320
silver-screen stars thanks to his work as an actor&nbsp;
and stuntman in martial arts and action movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.800
it seemed inevitable that Chan&nbsp;
would take over Hollywood.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.680
In 1998, he took a giant leap toward that&nbsp;
feat when he scored a role in his first big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.640
international blockbuster, Rush Hour. Playing&nbsp;
opposite Chris Tucker in the action-comedy film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.600
Chan became instantly recognizable to fans all&nbsp;
across the globe. From there, he followed up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.880
first Rush Hour with a handful of sequels and many&nbsp;
other American projects like The Spy Next Door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.240
all three Kung Fu Panda movies, a 2010 remake&nbsp;
of The Karate Kid starring Jaden Smith.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.120
It's undeniable that Chan is one&nbsp;
of the biggest stars in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.840
but he's largely disappeared from the American&nbsp;
spotlight, though he's still churning out films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.720
in his native Hong Kong. Here's the real reason&nbsp;
Jackie Chan stopped filming American movies.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.320
In an October 2020 interview with Filmelier,&nbsp;
which was translated into English by MovieWeb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.040
Chan revealed the reason why he's been sticking to&nbsp;
movies made in Asia lately. As he told the outlet,

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.800
"[I've] never left America, [but I haven't&nbsp;
been able to] find the right script."

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.520
Unfortunately for Chan, he seems to get&nbsp;
pigeonholed a lot. Chan referenced the seven-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.680
gap between 2010's Karate Kid and 2017's The&nbsp;
Foreigner co-starring Pierce Brosnan, and said,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.280
"All these years after The Karate&nbsp;
Kid, I received so many scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.200
but the role is [always] a Hong Kong&nbsp;
policeman. That's why it took me seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.120
years to accept a role in another&nbsp;
Hollywood film, with The Foreigner."

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.200
"I said can I have something&nbsp;
different like Mamma Mia?"

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.280
Ultimately, Chan wants to diversify his roles&nbsp;
and get into more dramatic work. During the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.680
same interview, Chan explained that his roles in&nbsp;
The Karate Kid and The Foreigner are the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.480
parts he's seeking out these days. In The Karate&nbsp;
Kid, Chan played a maintenance man who's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.960
a kung fu master; he becomes the mentor to&nbsp;
a 12-year-old boy who recently moved to Hong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.240
Kong and wants to learn to defend himself. Chan's&nbsp;
role in The Foreigner was just as interesting. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.680
played an ex-special forces operative who became a&nbsp;
restaurateur in London following the Vietnam War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.720
However, when his teenage daughter dies in a&nbsp;
terrorist bombing, Chan's character seeks revenge.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.880
These parts in Hollywood films allowed Chan&nbsp;
to explore new territory, which is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.960
he wants to do more of on screen. If the&nbsp;
right Hollywood movie script comes along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.080
Chan would presumably jump at the chance. That&nbsp;
just hasn't happened yet, as he told Filmelier,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.760
"I want to make sure that every year the&nbsp;
audience can see so many different sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.560
of Jackie Chan [...] I would like audiences&nbsp;
to consider me as an actor who can do action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.000
not just as an action star. I&nbsp;
don't like to repeat myself."

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.120
This isn't the first big announcement Chan has&nbsp;
made about the future of his career. At the 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.080
Cannes Film Festival, the icon admitted that he&nbsp;
was stepping away from action movies. He revealed,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.120
"I will ask my body how long&nbsp;
I can go [...] I tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.240
I am not young anymore [...]&nbsp;
I am really, really tired."

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.880
It also isn't the first time that Chan has&nbsp;
discussed his desire for audiences to see him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.760
as more than just an action movie star. During the&nbsp;
Cannes Film Festival press conference for Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.240
Zodiac in 2012, the actor shared that&nbsp;
he wanted to show the world "the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.880
Jackie Chan." To do that, Chan's plan&nbsp;
was to move away from action-heavy roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.360
and into more dramatic territory. He explained,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.360
"I don't just want to be an action star, I&nbsp;
want to be a true actor [...] I want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.240
an Asian Robert De Niro. I want to get rid of&nbsp;
my image. So, for the last ten years I've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.760
other films like The Karate Kid,&nbsp;
where I'd rather play an old man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.480
I want the audience to know I'm not just&nbsp;
about fighting or comedy, also I can act."

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.720
With all this considered, Chan also&nbsp;
recently teased a fourth Rush Hour movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.840
so it seems that nothing's off the table just yet.

